Certainly! Below is an example of how you might implement a simple raycasting algorithm in GLSL, specifically using the WebGL 2 API which supports both vertex and fragment shaders.

### Vertex Shader

The vertex shader will pass through the position of each vertex without any transformation since we're not doing any complex transformations here.

```glsl
#version 300 es
in vec4 a_position;
out vec2 v_texCoord;

void main() {
    gl_Position = a_position;
    v_texCoord = a_position.xy * 0.5 + 0.5; // Normalize to [0,1] range
}
```

### Fragment Shader

The fragment shader will perform the actual raycasting logic. This example assumes that your scene data (like walls or objects) is stored in a texture map where different colors represent different types of surfaces.

```glsl
#version 300 es
precision highp float;

uniform sampler2D u_textureMap;
uniform vec2 u_resolution;
uniform float u_rayStepSize;

in vec2 v_texCoord;
out vec4 fragColor;

// Simple function to cast a ray from the center of the screen towards the point on the screen.
vec4 castRay(vec2 origin, vec2 direction) {
    float t = 0.0;
    while (true) {
        vec2 p = origin + t * direction;
        if (p.x < 0.0 || p.x > 1.0 || p.y < 0.0 || p.y > 1.0) break; // Out of bounds

        vec4 colorAtPoint = texture(u_textureMap, p);

        if (colorAtPoint.a == 0.0) continue; // No surface at this point

        return colorAtPoint;

        t += u_rayStepSize;
    }

    return vec4(0.0); // Return black if no intersection found
}

void main() {
    vec2 aspectRatio = u_resolution / min(u_resolution.x, u_resolution.y);
    vec2 dir = normalize(vec2(v_texCoord.x - 0.5, -(v_texCoord.y - 0.5)) * aspectRatio);

    vec4 color = castRay(vec2(0.5), dir);
    fragColor = color;
}
```

This code provides a basic implementation of raycasting in GLSL. The `castRay` function casts a ray from the center of the screen towards the pixel being rendered, checking against a texture map (`u_textureMap`) to determine what it hits. If it finds a non-transparent pixel, it returns its color; otherwise, it continues casting until it goes out of bounds.

Note: In practice, you would need to set up your WebGL context properly, create buffers, compile shaders, link them into a program, and then use that program to render your scene. Additionally, you'd typically have more sophisticated collision detection and possibly lighting calculations depending on the complexity of your scene.

