Here's an example of reading a CSV file using plain JavaScript:

```javascript
// Function to fetch and parse a CSV file
function readCSVFile(file) {
  // Create a FileReader object
  const reader = new FileReader();

  // Define what happens when the file has been loaded successfully
  reader.onload = function(event) {
    // Get the content of the file as text
    const csvText = event.target.result;

    // Split the text into lines
    const lines = csvText.split('\n');

    // Process each line
    lines.forEach(line => {
      // Split each line into fields based on commas
      const fields = line.split(',');

      // Do something with the fields...
      console.log(fields);
    });
  };

  // Read the contents of the file as text
  reader.readAsText(file);
}

// Example usage:
const inputElement = document.getElementById('csvInput');
inputElement.addEventListener('change', function(event) {
  const file = event.target.files[0];
  if (file) {
    readCSVFile(file);
  }
});
```

### Explanation:

1. **Creating a FileReader Object**: The `FileReader` interface allows web applications to asynchronously read the contents of files stored on the user's computer, using File or Blob objects to specify the file or data to read.

2. **Defining onload Event Handler**: When the file is loaded successfully, the `onload` event handler is triggered. Inside this handler, we get the content of the file as text (`event.target.result`).

3. **Splitting Text into Lines**: We split the entire text content into individual lines using the newline character `\n`.

4. **Processing Each Line**: For each line, we further split it into fields using the comma `,` delimiter. This gives us an array of values for each row in the CSV file.

5. **Reading the File**: Finally, we use `reader.readAsText(file)` to start reading the selected file as text.

6. **Example Usage**: An HTML `<input type="file">` element is used to allow users to select a CSV file. Once a file is chosen, its `change` event triggers the `readCSVFile` function.

This approach works well for simple CSV files without headers or special characters that might interfere with parsing. If your CSV files have headers or require more sophisticated handling, consider using libraries like [PapaParse](https://www.papaparse.com/), which provide robust features for parsing CSV data.

To extract text from a document using JavaScript, you can use various libraries and APIs depending on the type of document (e.g., PDF, Word, HTML). Below is an example of how to extract text from an HTML document using plain JavaScript.

### Extracting Text from an HTML Document

```javascript
function extractTextFromHTML(html) {
    // Create a temporary div element
    const tempDiv = document.createElement('div');

    // Set the innerHTML of the temporary div to the provided HTML content
    tempDiv.innerHTML = html;

    // Get all text nodes within the temporary div
    const textNodes = tempDiv.textContent || tempDiv.innerText || '';

    return textNodes.trim();
}

// Example usage:
const htmlContent = `
    <html>
        <body>
            <h1>Hello, World!</h1>
            <p>This is a sample paragraph.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Item 1</li>
                <li>Item 2</li>
            </ul>
        </body>
    </html>
`;

const extractedText = extractTextFromHTML(htmlContent);
console.log(extractedText);
```

### Explanation:
- **`document.createElement('div')`**: Creates a new `div` element in memory.
- **`tempDiv.innerHTML = html`**: Sets the inner HTML of the temporary `div` to the provided HTML content.
- **`tempDiv.textContent` or `tempDiv.innerText`**: Retrieves the text content of the `div`, including all child elements' text.
- **`trim()`**: Removes any leading or trailing whitespace from the extracted text.

### Output:
```
Hello, World! This is a sample paragraph. Item 1 Item 2
```

### Notes:
- This method works well for extracting text from HTML documents.
- If you need to extract text from other types of documents (e.g., PDFs, Word files), you would need to use specialized libraries like [pdf.js](https://mozilla.github.io/pdf.js/) for PDFs or [mammoth.js](https://github.com/mwilliamson/mammoth.js) for Word documents.

