1. Add the necessary dependencies: You will need to add the following dependencies to your project's build file (e.g., Maven or Gradle):

   - `io.kubernetes:client-java` for interacting with the Kubernetes API
   - `org.apache.httpcomponents:httpclient` for making HTTP requests

2. Create a class that encapsulates the logic for reading pod logs. Here is an example implementation using the Kubernetes Java client and Apache HttpClient:

```java
import io.kubernetes.client.openapi.ApiClient;
import io.kubernetes.client.openapi.Configuration;
import io.kubernetes.client.openapi.apis.CoreV1Api;
import io.kubernetes.client.util.Config;
import org.apache.http.HttpResponse;
import org.apache.http.client.methods.HttpGet;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.CloseableHttpClient;
import org.apache.http.impl.client.HttpClients;

public class KubernetesLogReader {

    private final String kubeConfigPath;
    private final CoreV1Api coreV1Api;

    public KubernetesLogReader(String kubeConfigPath) throws Exception {
        this.kubeConfigPath = kubeConfigPath;
        ApiClient apiClient = Config.defaultClient();
        Configuration.setDefaultApiClient(apiClient);
        this.coreV1Api = new CoreV1Api();
    }

    public String getPodLogs(String namespace, String podName) throws Exception {
        // Get the container name from the pod
        var pod = coreV1Api.readNamespacedPod(podName, namespace, null);
        String containerName = pod.getSpec().getContainers().get(0).getName();

        // Construct the URL for the pod logs endpoint
        String url = "http://localhost:8001/api/v1/namespaces/" + namespace + "/pods/" + podName + "/log?container=" + containerName;

        try (CloseableHttpClient httpClient = HttpClients.createDefault()) {
            HttpGet request = new HttpGet(url);
            HttpResponse response = httpClient.execute(request);

            if (response.getStatusLine().getStatusCode() == 200) {
                return EntityUtils.toString(response.getEntity());
            } else {
                throw new RuntimeException("Failed to fetch pod logs: " + response.getStatusLine().getReasonPhrase());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

3. Use the `KubernetesLogReader` class to read pod logs:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    String kubeConfigPath = "~/.kube/config"; // Path to your kubeconfig file
    KubernetesLogReader logReader = new KubernetesLogReader(kubeConfigPath);

    String namespace = "default";
    String podName = "my-pod";

    String logs = logReader.getPodLogs(namespace, podName);
    System.out.println(logs);
}
```

