```javascript
function retryRequest(requestFunction, maxRetries = 3) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const attempt = (retryCount) => {
            requestFunction()
                .then(resolve)
                .catch((error) => {
                    if (retryCount < maxRetries) {
                        console.log(`Retrying... Attempt ${retryCount + 1}`);
                        setTimeout(() => attempt(retryCount + 1), 1000); // Retry after 1 second delay
                    } else {
                        reject(error);
                    }
                });
        };

        attempt(0);
    });
}

// Example usage:
const makeRequest = () => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        // Simulate a network request with a random success or failure
        const isSuccess = Math.random() > 0.5;
        if (isSuccess) {
            resolve("Request succeeded!");
        } else {
            reject(new Error("Request failed!"));
        }
    });
};

retryRequest(makeRequest)
    .then((result) => console.log(result))
    .catch((error) => console.error(error));
```

In this code snippet, `retryRequest` is a higher-order function that takes another function (`requestFunction`) as its argument and returns a new promise. The inner `attempt` function handles the actual retry logic recursively.

The `makeRequest` function simulates a network request by randomly deciding whether it succeeds or fails. You can replace this with your own asynchronous operation.

When calling `retryRequest`, you pass in the `makeRequest` function along with any additional options like `maxRetries`. If the initial call to `requestFunction` fails, subsequent attempts will be made until either the maximum number of retries is reached or the request succeeds. Each retry has a one-second delay between attempts using `setTimeout`.

